;; Kestrel — a fictional company, used to fill a workspace with something
;; worth looking at.
;;
;; A six-person SaaS startup: small enough that one person can hold the whole
;; company in their head while reading it, and shaped so the review beat below
;; has somewhere to happen. Everything here is INVENTED.
;;
;; Every `:at` is narrative time and the seeder writes it as `:db/txInstant`
;; (see `demo.scenario/provenance`), so the company has a real history to scrub
;; through rather than one seeding burst. Stores install at the earliest of
;; these instants — a cut before install throws on lookup refs (task #33).
;;
;; FOUR KBs, one per lane, because a proposal fork is per SCOPE: four agents
;; writing into four knowledge bases produce four independently reviewable
;; items on one card, which is the review beat.

{:company "Kestrel"
 :tagline "Inventory and ordering for independent pharmacies"

 :kbs
 [{:name "Engineering"
   :pages
   [{:title "Incident Response"
     :at "2026-02-11T17:20:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>An incident is any failure a customer can feel. Silent failures count double: if we only learn about it because someone complained, the detection gap is the incident.</p>"
      "<p>First responder does three things in order: stop the bleeding, measure the blast radius, then write down what happened. Fixing before measuring is how we lose the account list.</p>"
      "<p>Anything touching money or customer records goes to a human before it lands. See [[Change Review]] — agents propose, they do not merge.</p>"
      "<p>Every incident gets a written timeline, even the ones resolved in ten minutes. [[Postmortem Template]] is the shape.</p>"]}
    {:title "Webhook Handling"
     :at "2026-03-04T16:15:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Stripe is the source of truth for payment, never our own database. We provision on the event, not on the redirect — a customer who closes the tab has still paid.</p>"
      "<p><strong>checkout.session.completed</strong> carries <code>status: \"complete\"</code>. <strong>invoice.paid</strong> carries <code>status: \"paid\"</code>. These are different strings for the same business fact and both must provision.</p>"
      "<p>Every handler is idempotent on <code>client_reference_id</code>. Stripe retries, and a double-provision is cheaper to prevent than to unpick.</p>"
      "<p>A handler that returns 200 without doing its work is worse than one that throws. Throw, let Stripe retry, and let the alert fire.</p>"]}
    {:title "Change Review"
     :at "2026-02-11T18:05:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Agents work on branches. Nothing an agent writes is live until a human accepts it.</p>"
      "<p>Review the change, not the description of the change. If you cannot see what actually moved, do not accept it.</p>"
      "<p>Refusing part of a proposal is normal and costs nothing. A gate that never refuses is not a gate.</p>"]}
    {:title "Postmortem Template"
     :at "2026-02-11T18:30:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p><strong>What happened</strong> — one paragraph, no jargon, written for someone who was asleep.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Detection gap</strong> — when it started, when we knew, and why those differ.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Blast radius</strong> — accounts affected, money involved, with the query that produced the number.</p>"
      "<p><strong>What we changed</strong> — the fix, and the thing that would have caught it sooner.</p>"]}]}

  {:name "Customers"
   :pages
   [{:title "Account Lifecycle"
     :at "2026-02-18T21:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>An account is <em>charged</em> when Stripe confirms payment and <em>provisioned</em> when the workspace exists and the owner can sign in. The gap between them should be seconds.</p>"
      "<p>A charged account that is not provisioned is our most expensive failure: the customer has paid and cannot use what they bought. Treat it as a P1 regardless of volume.</p>"
      "<p>Plans are Starter, Team and Business. Plan does not change the urgency — a Starter customer locked out is as locked out as a Business one.</p>"]}
    {:title "Reconciliation"
     :at "2026-02-18T21:40:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Charged-but-not-provisioned is a query, not a spreadsheet: accounts with a charge timestamp and no provisioning timestamp inside a window.</p>"
      "<p>Run it against the window the incident actually covers, not the whole month. A wider window inflates the number with accounts that were fine.</p>"
      "<p>Anyone already credited leaves the list. Double-refunding is its own incident.</p>"]}]}

  {:name "Finance"
   :pages
   [{:title "Credit Policy"
     :at "2026-01-22T16:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>When our failure costs a customer access, we credit the affected period without being asked. Waiting to be asked converts a service failure into a trust failure.</p>"
      "<p>A credit is a full month for anything over four hours of lost access, prorated below that. We do not means-test by plan size.</p>"
      "<p>Credits are posted to the ledger as they are issued, never batched at month end — the books should be able to answer &quot;what do we owe right now&quot; at any moment.</p>"]}
    {:title "Spend Authority"
     :at "2026-01-22T16:30:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p><strong>Under 5 000 SEK</strong> — an agent may prepare and post without approval, inside an approved policy like [[Credit Policy]].</p>"
      "<p><strong>5 000 – 50 000 SEK</strong> — an agent prepares the entry, a founder approves before it posts. This is the common case and the approval is the point, not a formality.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Above 50 000 SEK</strong> — both founders, in writing, with the consequence of not doing it stated.</p>"
      "<p>Preparing is not spending. An agent may always draft the entry; posting it is the gated step.</p>"]}]}

  {:name "Comms"
   :pages
   [{:title "Customer Communication"
     :at "2026-01-30T20:00:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p>Tell customers what happened, what it cost them, and what we did. In that order, in their words, not ours.</p>"
      "<p>Never speculate about cause in a customer-facing message. &quot;We are investigating&quot; is honest; a guess that turns out wrong is a second incident.</p>"
      "<p><strong>Never admit legal liability in a support message.</strong> Describe what happened and what we are doing. Words like &quot;our failure&quot;, &quot;we are responsible for&quot; and &quot;we accept liability&quot; are for our lawyer to choose, not for us and not for an agent. This has bitten companies our size badly.</p>"
      "<p>Anything going to more than ten customers at once is reviewed by a founder before it sends.</p>"]}
    {:title "Notice Templates"
     :at "2026-01-30T20:40:00Z"
     :blocks
     ["<p><strong>Service interruption</strong> — what was unavailable, for how long, what we did, what the customer needs to do (usually nothing).</p>"
      "<p><strong>Billing correction</strong> — what was charged, what it should have been, when the correction lands, and the reference to look for on their statement.</p>"
      "<p>Subject lines say the thing. &quot;An update on your account&quot; makes people assume the worst.</p>"]}]}]

