 ;; A book with a history, so credits post into a real ledger rather than an
 ;; empty one. SEK: Kestrel is Swedish, and the starter book's USD is a default
 ;; that makes the verbs reachable, not a claim about the tenant.
 ;;
 ;; THE BOOK AND THE CUSTOMER LIST MUST TELL THE SAME STORY. Someone reads the
 ;; Accounting view and the customer records in the same session; if the ledger
 ;; describes a company seven times the size of the roster, the fixture is
 ;; teaching a wrong intuition about what these views mean together.
 ;;
 ;; So the revenue lines are DERIVED from the roster in `incident.edn`, not
 ;; chosen: 46 accounts on Starter/Team/Business at 299/799/1499 SEK
 ;; (29900/79900/149900 öre) — 22 + 16 + 8 — bill 31 354 SEK a month. June is
 ;; exactly that, so anyone who adds up the customer list arrives at the number
 ;; in the book. May is the same roster three signups shorter (20/15/8 =
 ;; 29 957 SEK), which is why the two months differ at all.
 ;;
 ;; The rest follows the same scale. Hosting at 4 800 SEK/month is what 46
 ;; accounts cost to run, and it sits just under the 5 000 SEK an agent may
 ;; post unsupervised ([[Spend Authority]]) — while the incident's 27 659 SEK
 ;; of exposure lands squarely in the founder-approval band, which is the whole
 ;; reason Snipe has to stop and ask. Opening equity is the founders' capital
 ;; and is left alone: it is not a claim about trading volume.
 ;;
 ;; Trial balance after these six entries (it must sum to zero):
 ;;   Assets:Bank        536 511.00     (480 000 + 29 957 + 31 354 - 4 800)
 ;;   Equity:Opening    -480 000.00
 ;;   Expenses:General      4 800.00
 ;;   Income:Sales        -61 311.00
 ;; Assets:Receivable nets to zero — each month's revenue is settled in full —
 ;; so it carries no row, as before.
